/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_PackageConsumer Package Consumer Validation
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Delivery
 * @brief Prove that an exported Release package works from a separate CMake project.
 *
 * A successful Foundation build does not prove that its installed package is
 * usable. Headers may be missing, the imported target may point to the wrong
 * archive, or a header-only smoke program may accidentally pass without ever
 * linking Foundation. The repository-owned fixture under
 * `tests/PackageConsumer` closes that gap without depending on another product
 * repository.
 *
 * MCC has independently consumed Foundation and remains valuable downstream
 * integration evidence. It does not replace this fixture: Foundation must be
 * able to validate its own package in CI even when MCC is unavailable or at a
 * different revision.
 *
 * @section FoundationPackageConsumerCommand One-Command Validation
 *
 * Export and validate the preset's default Release package:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test-package.sh macos_arm64 --fresh --parallel 8
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/test-package.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh -Parallel 8
 * @endcode
 *
 * The scripts perform these steps:
 *
 * 1. invoke the normal export workflow unless package reuse was requested;
 * 2. obtain the generator, native compiler, and architecture from the selected
 *    preset's configured build tree;
 * 3. recreate `build/package-consumer/<preset>` so stale consumer objects
 *    cannot hide a package defect;
 * 4. configure `tests/PackageConsumer` with the exported prefix as its only
 *    allowed Foundation search path;
 * 5. build the consumer in Release through `Foundation::Foundation`;
 * 6. execute its registered CTest and reject a zero-test result.
 *
 * The consumer is deliberately a standalone CMake project. It is not added by
 * Foundation's root `add_subdirectory(tests)`, has no source-tree include path,
 * and uses this package lookup:
 *
 * @code{cmake}
 * find_package(Foundation 1.0 CONFIG REQUIRED
 *     PATHS "${FOUNDATION_PACKAGE_PREFIX}"
 *     NO_DEFAULT_PATH
 * )
 * target_link_libraries(FoundationPackageConsumer PRIVATE Foundation::Foundation)
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationPackageConsumerCoverage What It Proves
 *
 * `main.cpp` includes the complete `Foundation.h` umbrella. A separate
 * translation unit includes only `Foundation/Time/Frequency.h` and
 * `Foundation/Time/Period.h`. Keeping the include styles in separate source
 * files prevents one style from masking missing dependencies in the other.
 *
 * The selective unit calls `Frequency::GetPeriod()` and
 * `Period::GetFrequency()`. Those functions are implemented outside the
 * headers, so a successful executable link proves that the imported target
 * supplied the Foundation Release archive. Merely compiling a template or
 * `constexpr` API would not provide that evidence.
 *
 * This fixture currently requires the default package with Math and Time
 * enabled. Feature-variant package validation belongs to `PKG-002` and must
 * select assertions compatible with the exported feature set.
 *
 * @section FoundationPackageConsumerReuse Reusing an Existing Package
 *
 * Avoid a second export after manually producing the package:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh
 * ./scripts/test-package.sh macos_arm64 --skip-export
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh
 * ./scripts/test-package.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -SkipExport
 * @endcode
 *
 * To inspect a different existing prefix, combine package reuse with an
 * explicit location:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test-package.sh macos_arm64 \
 *     --skip-export --package /absolute/path/to/Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/test-package.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 `
 *     -SkipExport -Package C:\packages\Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * The selected preset must already be configured because its build cache is
 * the source of the consumer's generator, compiler, and architecture. The
 * custom package is not modified.
 *
 * @section FoundationPackageConsumerLimits Native Execution Boundary
 *
 * This workflow executes the resulting program and therefore accepts only a
 * native preset. `macos_x64` execution on Apple Silicon requires Rosetta or an
 * Intel host. AVR, STM32, and other cross-compiled packages need a dedicated
 * firmware consumer, compatible startup/runtime/linker configuration, and
 * hardware or emulator execution. A host-side CTest must not be reported as
 * embedded target evidence.
 *
 * @section FoundationPackageConsumerCI CI Contract
 *
 * Native GitHub Actions jobs should call the repository script after Debug and
 * Release unit tests. CI YAML selects the runner and preset; it must not
 * duplicate the export/find-package/build/test sequence. A passing job records
 * both package creation and execution of a separately configured consumer.
 */
